### Product intended purpose and reasonable foreseeable use

The system controls physical passage through doors in a commercial office
building by validating a credential presented at a door-mounted reader,
releasing or holding the door, and recording the attempt.

- **[PUR-1]** Intended use: only a holder of a valid, currently-enrolled
  credential may pass a controlled door; every grant and denial is logged.
- **[PUR-2]** Facility admins enrol and revoke credentials and review the
  access log; they are the only privileged operators.
- **[PUR-3]** Foreseeable use: doors also release on a request-to-exit sensor
  from the secure side, and on fire-alarm override — both bypass the credential
  check by design (life-safety takes precedence over access control).
- **[PUR-4]** Foreseeable misuse: tailgating — an unauthorised person follows an
  authorised one through a door that is already released.
- **[PUR-5]** Foreseeable misuse: a lost or stolen credential is presented
  before the admin revokes it.

### Product functions

- **[FN-1]** Validates a presented credential and decides grant or deny against
  its access rights.
- **[FN-2]** Releases the door on a grant for a bounded unlock window, then
  re-secures it.
- **[FN-3]** Releases the door without a credential check on a request-to-exit
  signal and on fire-alarm override.
- **[FN-4]** Records every grant, denial, and door event for audit.
- **[FN-5]** Enrols and revokes credentials and sets access rights (which
  credential opens which door, and when).
- **[FN-6]** Decides grant/deny locally at each door so access continues during
  loss of connectivity.
- **[FN-7]** Authenticates administrators before any enrolment, revocation, or
  log access.

### Product operational environment

- **[ENV-1]** Readers are mounted on the *unsecured* side of each door, physically
  reachable by anyone in semi-public corridors, lobbies, and entrances.
- **[ENV-2]** The door-release path on the secure side (lock wiring, exit sensor)
  is assumed physically protected; the reader side is not.
- **[ENV-3]** Components communicate over the building IP network, which is shared
  with other building/IT systems and is **not** assumed trustworthy.
- **[ENV-4]** Management is operated from a physically secured area (locked IT/comms
  room) with restricted access.
- **[ENV-5]** Remote management and updates traverse the public internet.
- **[ENV-6]** The fire-alarm system is an external system trusted to assert door
  release [FN-3]; the integrity of that signal is outside this product's control.
- **[ENV-7]** Egress behaviour on power/connectivity loss is set per door:
  fail-safe (release for free egress) on normal occupancy routes, fail-secure
  (remain locked) where security or regulation requires. The choice per controlled
  door is stated, not assumed.
- **[ENV-8]** Power model is set per door, not shared: mains-wired doors vs.
  self-contained battery locksets ("in handle") that operate independently of
  building power — sets the availability threat (mains outage vs. battery
  depletion / scheduled replacement).

### Product user description

- **[USR-1]** Facility admins — building-management staff who enrol/revoke
  credentials, set access rights, and review logs [PUR-2]. Trusted but not security
  specialists; the expected failure is misconfiguration (over-broad rights, slow
  revocation [PUR-5]), not malice. Manage via a cloud portal, so [ENV-5] applies
  to them (RDPS-dependent).
- **[USR-2]** End users — staff who present a badge and nothing more [FN-1]. No
  security training; predictable behaviour includes tailgating [PUR-4], lending or
  sharing badges, and delayed reporting of a lost badge.
- **[USR-3]** Installers / maintainers — contracted field technicians who commission
  and service units [SC-4]. Transient physical access to door hardware; not direct
  employees, so their trust is bounded.
- **[USR-4]** Security / IT operations — monitor system health and respond to access
  alerts. On a smaller site this role may be absent, leaving alerts unwatched.

### Lifecycle context

Non-operational phases the product passes through, covered lightly here (per EN
40000 Clause 7 — secure development, production/distribution, and decommissioning).
A phase graduates to its own threat model if it warrants one; until then a threat
references these tags the same way it references `[ENV]`.

#### Design & development

- **[DEV-1]** The product is threat-modelled (this document) and code-reviewed
  before release.
- **[DEV-2]** Keys and credentials are generated with vetted libraries; no secrets
  are hardcoded in firmware.
- **[DEV-3]** Third-party and OSS components are tracked in an SBOM and monitored
  for known vulnerabilities.

#### Production & distribution

- **[SC-1]** Readers, controllers, and locksets are built by third-party OEMs and
  integrated by the manufacturer.
- **[SC-2]** Firmware is signed at the factory; the signing key is held by the
  manufacturer and not present on shipped units.
- **[SC-3]** Units ship through third-party logistics to site; tamper-evident
  packaging is _fill in: applied / not applied_.
- **[SC-4]** Field installers commission units and set the initial configuration
  and enrolment; they are contracted, not direct employees.

#### Decommissioning & destruction

- **[EOL-1]** Decommissioned units and their stored credential/access data and keys
  are wiped or physically destroyed before disposal or reuse.
- **[EOL-2]** Removed units and their credentials are revoked centrally so a
  recovered unit cannot be reused.

